            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Collaboration with Marie Curie</strong><br>\nIn 1894, Pierre Curie met Marie Sklodowska, who would become his wife and research partner. Their collaborative work on radioactivity, a term they coined, led to groundbreaking findings. Together, they conducted pioneering research, which would later earn them the Nobel Prize in Physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nIn 1903, Pierre and Marie Curie, along with Henri Becquerel, were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics for their work on radioactivity. This accolade recognized their joint research on the phenomenon and the discovery of new elements, polonium and radium.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Teaching Career</strong><br>\nBesides his research, Pierre Curie held a professorship at the University of Paris. He was dedicated to teaching and believed in nurturing the scientific curiosity of his students. He became a respected educator who inspired many young scientists.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
