          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Values</strong><br>\nPierre Curie was known not only for his scientific rigor but also for his personal integrity and humanitarian values. He was not interested in wealth from his discoveries, focusing instead on scientific progress and its benefits to humanity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Accidents</strong><br>\nThroughout his life, Pierre Curie was often ill due to his exposure to radioactive materials. He also faced several accidents, as his health was compromised by his intensive laboratory work. Despite this, he continued his research until his untimely death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nPierre Curie died in a tragic street accident in Paris in 1906, at the age of 46. His legacy, however, lives on through the continued relevance of his scientific work, the Curie Institutes founded by his wife, and the ongoing respect for the Curie name in scientific communities around the world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
